<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Tai Federation</h1><p class="st-meta">1948 CE – 1954 CE · capital Lai Chau</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A French-backed autonomous federation of Tai peoples in north-western Vietnam, led by Deo Van Long and ended by the fall of Dien Bien Phu.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sip-song-chau-tai">Sip Song Chau Tai</a>, 'twelve Tai lands', of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-river-asia">Black River</a> region had long been ruled by Tai chiefly families such as the Deo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lai-chau">Lai Chau</a>. In 1948 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> formally created the Tai Federation under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deo-van-long">Deo Van Long</a>, who had backed the French against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a>, as part of its strategy of ethnic autonomous zones.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The federation was governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lai-chau">Lai Chau</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deo-van-long">Deo Van Long</a> with Tai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hmong-people">Hmong</a> and other upland chiefs, under French military protection, and fielded Tai battalions alongside French units. It was nominally attached to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bao-dai">Bao Dai's</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-vietnam">State of Vietnam</a> from 1950. Opium was an important part of the local economy and of French financing in the region.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> offensives overran much of the area in 1952 to 1953, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lai-chau">Lai Chau</a> was evacuated before the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dien-bien-phu">Dien Bien Phu</a> in 1954. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-geneva">Geneva</a> Accords gave the region to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/democratic-republic-of-vietnam">North Vietnam</a>, which created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tay-bac-autonomous-region">Thai-Meo Autonomous Region</a>, later renamed Tay Bac; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deo-van-long">Deo Van Long</a> went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tai-federation.jpg" alt="Tai Federation" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Germenfer · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Tai_Dam_People.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1948 CE – 1954 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lai Chau</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Deo Van Long</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Folk religion, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tai Dam, Tai Don</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sip Song Chau Tai, Fédération Thaï</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1951&amp;focus=tai-federation" aria-label="Tai Federation on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7501 1362 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tai Federation lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7821 1618h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7821 1618h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Vietnam. <a href="/atlas?year=1951&amp;focus=tai-federation">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

